# SLAMTEC RPLIDAR A1M8.
#
# The sensor is attached through a Silicon Labs CP2102 USB-to-UART bridge
# (10c4:ea60), so it enumerates as /dev/ttyUSB*. That name is assigned in
# enumeration order and will change as soon as the Arduino motor controller
# is attached alongside it. Code must open /dev/rplidar, never /dev/ttyUSB0.
#
# GOTCHA: the RPLIDAR's own serial number (reported by GET_INFO over the
# protocol, e.g. 6946595DF298E6A5C998EAC093ED87C8) is NOT visible to udev.
# udev only sees the USB descriptors of the CP2102 bridge, whose serial
# string is the generic unprogrammed value "0001". It is matched anyway so
# that a USB-serial device with a properly programmed serial cannot claim
# this symlink, but it does NOT disambiguate a second unprogrammed CP2102.
#
# A second unprogrammed CP2102 did appear: the Arduino motor controller (a
# Keyestudio Uno clone) uses the same bridge, 10c4:ea60, serial "0001".
# Checked on the Pi 2026-10-20: with both plugged in, both matched the old
# serial-based rule and both claimed /dev/rplidar. So the rule now matches
# the physical USB port instead, and the LiDAR must stay in that port:
#
#   KERNELS "1-1.4"   the LiDAR (this file)
#   KERNELS "1-1.2"   the Arduino (99-heron-arduino.rules)
#
# Label both ports on the Pi. A device moved to another port loses its name,
# which is the safe failure: the program finds no device, rather than the
# wrong one. To find a port's KERNELS value, plug one device in alone and
# run:
#   udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyUSB0 | grep KERNELS
# On a Pi 3B, 1-1.1 is the Ethernet adapter and 1-1.2 to 1-1.5 the four
# USB sockets.
#
# All keys sit on the same parent device (the USB device at 1-1.4), as udev
# requires within one rule.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="1-1.4", SYMLINK+="rplidar", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
